STAR1001 — CTF Write-up

Overview

The challenge was a web-based CTF challenge running on:

http://34.116.80.78:9981/

The goal was to inspect the web application, identify a suspicious encoded string, and decode it to retrieve the flag.

1. Initial Request

I started by sending a basic `GET` request to the root endpoint using Burp Suite:

GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: 34.116.80.78:9981

The server returned:


HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Server: nginx
Content-Type: text/html

The response body contained a simple HTML page.


2. Analyzing the HTML

The HTML did not contain the flag directly, but there was an interesting external resource:

<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">


Since external resources can sometimes contain useful information in CTF challenges, I requested the CSS file.

3. Inspecting `style.css`

Using Burp Repeater, I sent:

GET /style.css HTTP/1.1
Host: 34.116.80.78:9981
Connection: close


The response contained a suspicious string:

Q1NTQ1RGJTdCd2VfQlUxTFRfdGhpc19jaXR5X2Zyb21fcjBja19hbmRfUjAxMSU3RA

The character set and structure suggested that this could be Base64-encoded data.

4. Base64 Decoding

I decoded the string using:

echo 'Q1NTQ1RGJTdCd2VfQlUxTFRfdGhpc19jaXR5X2Zyb21fcjBja19hbmRfUjAxMSU3RA' | base64 -d

The result was:

CSSCTF%7Bwe_BU1LT_this_city_from_r0ck_and_R011%7D

The output was not yet the final flag because `%7B` and `%7D` are URL-encoded characters.



5. URL Decoding

After URL-decoding the result:


CSSCTF{we_BU1LT_this_city_from_r0ck_and_R011}


The flag was revealed.



6. Flag


CSSCTF{we_BU1LT_this_city_from_r0ck_and_R011}




Takeaways

This challenge was a good reminder to inspect **all referenced resources**, not just the main HTML response. Even when the main page appears to contain no useful information, files such as CSS and JavaScript may contain hidden clues.

It was also important not to stop after the first decoding step. The Base64-decoded output still contained URL encoding, requiring a second decoding step to obtain the final flag.
